practice.a:
First derivative: $p'(x)=\frac{2x}{x^2+1}$.
Second derivative using quotient rule:
$p''(x)=\frac{2(x^2+1)-2x(2x)}{(x^2+1)^2}
=\frac{2x^2+2-4x^2}{(x^2+1)^2}
=\frac{2-2x^2}{(x^2+1)^2}
=\frac{2(1-x^2)}{(x^2+1)^2}$.
Set $p''(x)=0$: numerator $2(1-x^2)=0 \Rightarrow x=\pm1$.
Check sign change: for $|x|<1$, $1-x^2>0$ so $p''>0$ (concave up); for $|x|>1$, $1-x^2<0$ so $p''<0$ (concave down).
Thus inflection points at $x=-1$ and $x=1$, with coordinates $(-1,\ln 2)$ and $(1,\ln 2)$.
